.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_ENABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_enable_sending \- turn sends on again on a dynamic queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_enable_sending(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Clears the flag that ccol_dynmq_disable_sending() set, so sends are
accepted again instead of returning ccol_not_permitted. A new queue
starts with sends on, so you only need this call after sends were
turned off.

It wakes every write-direction waiter on the queue (a ccol_select()
call, a ccol_select_timed() call or a ccol_event_loop registration),
and each of them scans again and sees the new state. While sends are
off the queue is ready for the write direction (see
ccol_dynmq_disable_sending(3)); with sends on, it is ready while it
holds fewer than ccol_max_elem_count messages.

Turning sends off and on again never drops anything: the messages that
were in the queue before stay as they were.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue that gets sends turned on
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - Sends are on again
ccol_invalid_args  - dq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_msg_count (3),
.BR ccol_circq_enable_sending (3)
